  "findings": [
    {
      "evidence": "tests/test_lifecycle_workers.py: assert_processes_gone loops on os.kill(pid, 0) until ProcessLookupError. The children are orphaned once their parent exits, so reaping depends on the host init. Checking /proc/<pid>/stat for state 'Z' would avoid this.",
      "id": "zombie-child-false-survivor",
      "severity": "advisory",
      "summary": "assert_processes_gone uses os.kill(pid, 0) to detect exit. A killed but unreaped (zombie) process still passes that probe. If the host has no init that reaps orphans (a container running as PID 1 without a reaper), the test would fail with 'outlived its process group' even though the group kill worked."
    },
    {
      "evidence": "tests/test_lifecycle_workers.py: LATE_CHILD near the top of the file and `late = (...)` in the FAKE_LLM 'sleeper' mode contain identical code. FAKE_LLM runs as a separate script, so it cannot import the constant directly, but the copies can drift apart.",
      "id": "duplicated-late-child-script",
      "severity": "advisory",
      "summary": "The pid-recording, 600 s sleeping child script is written out twice: as LATE_CHILD at module level, and as a separate `late` string inside FAKE_LLM."
    }
  ],
